{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>When a ruler sins</b> — this is the language of felicitation: happy is the generation whose ruler brings an atonement offering for his inadvertent sin — Torat Kohanim (Hova, chapter 5). <b>Or if it is made known to him</b> — if it is made known. Many instances of \"or\" (או) function like \"if\" (אִם), and likewise \"if\" (אִם) in place of \"or\" (או), and so too \"or it is known that it is a goring ox\" (Exodus 21:36) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 4:22). And Ibn Ezra wrote that \"אֲשֶׁר נָשִׂיא יֶחֱטָא\" is inverted — as though it said \"אֲשֶׁר יֶחֱטָא נָשִׂיא\" — and it is attached to what precedes it: \"and if the whole congregation of Israel sins inadvertently\" — as if it said, \"and if the one who sins is the ruler, or his sin is made known to him.\" Scripture took a shortened path. And the reason [for the phrase] is that it becomes known to the ruler on his own that he sinned, or [alternatively] \"or it was made known to him\" — another person who saw it informed him. And the grammatical analysis of \"הוֹדַע\" would be as a past-tense verb, like \"and it shall distress you\" (Deuteronomy 28:52), and the one who informs would be elliptical, like \"who bore you to Levi\" (Numbers 26:59). But none of this is necessary, for the uses of \"אֲשֶׁר\" are many. Some of them indicate time, such as \"כַּאֲשֶׁר בָּא יוֹסֵף אֶל אֶחָיו\" (Genesis 37:23), \"כַּאֲשֶׁר כִלּוּ לֶאֱכֹל אֶת הַשֶּׁבֶר\" (Genesis 43:2), and their parallels — so too here: כַּאֲשֶׁר נָשִׂיא יֶחֱטָא, with the כ of temporality elided. And so too \"אֶת הַבְּרָכָה אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ אֶל מִצְוֹת ה' אֱלֹהֵיכֶם\" (Deuteronomy 11:27) — כַּאֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ; \"אֲשֶׁר רְאִיתֶם אֶת מִצְרַיִם הַיּוֹם לֹא תֹסִיפוּ לִרְאֹתָם עוֹד\" (Exodus 14:13) — כַּאֲשֶׁר רְאִיתֶם; \"שִׁבְעַת יָמִים תֹּאכַל מַצּוֹת אֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִיךָ\" (Exodus 34:18) — כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִיךָ. And sometimes this word lacks a ב: \"וַיְסִרֶהָ מִגְּבִירָה אֲשֶׁר עָשְׂתָה מִפְלֶצֶת לָאֲשֵׁרָה\" (1 Kings 15:13) — בַּאֲשֶׁר עָשְׂתָה מִפְלֶצֶת לָאֲשֵׁרָה, in the sense of \"because,\" like \"בַּאֲשֶׁר אַתְּ אִשְׁתּוֹ\" (Genesis 39:9). And the sense of \"or it is made known to him\" attaches to \"and he incurs guilt\" (וְאָשֵׁם): it says that when he commits any of the commandments of YHWH that are not to be done and incurs guilt so that punishment falls upon him, or it becomes known to him and he attains atonement through an offering — the meaning is: either he is guilty, or he brings an offering and attains atonement. And the reason for \"his God\" (ה' אֱלֹהָיו) is to say that even though he is the king, the master, before whom no flesh-and-blood person stands in fear, he must fear YHWH his God, for He is the Master of masters; and likewise \"that he may learn to fear YHWH his God\" (Deuteronomy 17:19) — meaning that he should set his heart upon the fact that there is one above him who is his God, in whose hand are his life and his kingdom."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The citation 'Torat Kohanim (Hova, chapter 5)' follows the standard reference to Sifra, the halakhic midrash on Leviticus, tractate Hova."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:33.793713586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}